"MAS-on-the-Fly: Dynamic Adaptation of LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems at Test Time (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1)\nciteturn31895view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1\",\"lineno\":98}); Total lines: 494\nL66: Extensive experiments demonstrate that MASFly achieves state-of-the-art performance, most notably a 61.7% success rate on the TravelPlanner benchmark, while exhibiting strong task adaptability and robustness.\nL67: ## 1 Introduction\nL68: The remarkable advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed the field of artificial intelligence, showing remarkable abilities in reasoning and generation cite47†Achiam et al. (2023) ; cite48†Liu et al. (2024) ; cite49†Yang et al. (2025) . However, tackling complex, multi-faceted problems often exceeds the capacity of a single LLM cite50†Talebirad and Nadiri (2023) . This has led to the rapid emergence of LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) cite51†Hong et al. (2023) ; cite52†Wu et al.\nL69: (2024) , where multiple agents take on specialized roles and work collaboratively, demonstrating human-like collective intelligence for advanced problem-solving.\nL70: cite53†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Drawing inspiration from biological adaptation, MASFly achieves dynamic adaptation at test-time, enabling adaptive system generation via SOP instantiation and adaptive execution via process supervision.\nL71: Early LLM-based MASs, like MetaGPT cite51†Hong et al. (2023) and ChatDev cite54†Qian et al. (2023) , construct system through handcrafted standard operating procedures (SOPs). Later works explore automating partial components of MAS: some generate agent roles using LLMs ( e.g., AgentVerse cite55†Chen et al. (2023) ), while others learn communication topologies with external modules (e.g., GPTSwarm cite56†Zhuge et al. (2024) ).\nL72: Recent studies pursue fully automated MAS construction, either by prompting LLMs to search for a single “optimal” system within a predefined design space (e.g., AgentSquare cite57†Shang et al. (2024) ), or by training a meta agent to generate systems for different queries (e.g., MAS-GPT cite58†Ye et al. (2025) ).\nL73: However, these methods lack dynamic adaptation at test time: one-size-fits-all systems cannot adjust their structure to different queries, while training-based approaches require substantial data and training before test. Moreover, most existing systems emphasize construction while overlooking the capacity for adaptive adjustment during execution, leading to brittle open-loop pipelines that cannot recover from single agent failures or unexpected environment feedback.\nL74: These limitations point to a key missing capability: Can we enable MAS to adapt dynamically at test time — both in how the system is constructed and how it executes — without any additional training?\nL75: To address this, we draw inspiration from biological adaptation: organisms utilize a conserved genetic toolkit encoding evolutionary experience to express diverse traits suited to varying environments (phenotypic plasticity), and leverage regulatory systems for real-time adjustment to maintain stability amidst internal and external perturbations (homeostatic regulation), as illustrated in Figure cite59†1 .\nL76: Inspired by this, we propose MASFly, a novel multi-agent framework that achieves on-the-fly adaptation without extra training, covering both adaptive system generation and execution.\nL77: Specifically, to navigate the combinatorially large design space of MAS, we mirror the logic of plasticity through a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism for dynamic system generation. MASFly maintains an SOP repository that stores successful collaboration patterns across tasks, serving as the “genetic units” of the system.\nL78: Given a new query, a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) process selects relevant patterns and adaptively expresses them, instantiating tailored agent roles and communication structures. Furthermore, to handle the non-stationary multi-agent environment—where agent actions reshape the context and external shifts occur unexpectedly—we incorporate a mechanism of homeostatic regulation via experience-guided process supervision.\nL79: A global Watcher agent acts as a regulator, monitoring the system while referring to a personalized experience pool as guidance, enabling on-the-fly adjustments (e.g., replacing malfunctioning agents) and forming a resilient closed-loop execution process. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MASFly achieves state-of-the-art performance, most notably a 61.7% success rate on the TravelPlanner benchmark. The contributions are summarized as follows:\nL80:   * •\nL81: \nL82: We propose MASFly, a novel multi-agent framework enabling dynamic adaptation at test time without extra training, achieving both adaptive system generation and execution.\nL83: \nL84:   * •\nL85: \nL86: We design a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism for dynamic system generation, which leverages an SOP repository of successful collaboration patterns to construct tailored MAS.\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: We design an experience-guided process supervision mechanism, which integrates a global Watcher agent and a personalized experience pool for real-time adaptation during execution.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: \nL93: Extensive experiments across diverse tasks demonstrate that MASFly not only outperforms strong baselines, but also exhibits superior task adaptability and robustness.\nL94: ## 2 Related Work\nL95: \nL96: cite60†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of our framework MASFly, which consists of three stages: (1) SOP-Guided System Generation, (2) Process-Supervised Execution, and (3) Reflective Experience Distillation.\nL97: ### 2.1 LLM-based Multi-Agent System\nL98: The evolution of LLM-based MAS has undergone a transition from manually designed frameworks to increasingly automated and adaptive construction. Early systems, such as MetaGPT cite51†Hong et al. (2023) and ChatDev cite54†Qian et al. (2023) , rely heavily on handcrafted SOPs to configure agents roles and workflows. Subsequent research introduces semi-automated mechanisms. For example, AgentVerse cite55†Chen et al. (2023) , EvoAgent cite61†Yuan et al. (2024) , and MegaAgent cite62†Wang et al.\nL99: (2025) leverage LLMs to automatically generate diverse agent roles. Other studies such as GPTSwarm cite56†Zhuge et al. (2024) and G-Designer cite63†Zhang et al. (2024a) , which employ external models to learn the communication topology across agents.\nL100: Recently, the focus has shifted toward fully automated MAS generation driven by LLMs. These methods typically prompt the LLM to generate executable code that represents the entire system. Search-based methods (e.g., ADAS cite64†Hu et al. (2024) , AFlow cite65†Zhang et al. (2024b) , AgentSquare cite57†Shang et al. (2024) , SwarmAgentic cite66†Zhang et al. (2025c) use LLMs to identify an \"optimal\" system configuration within a predefined search space.\nL101: However, these methods rely on fixed system, lacking dynamic adaptability to diverse queries. Training-based approaches, such as MAS-GPT cite58†Ye et al. (2025) and FlowReasoner cite67†Gao et al. (2025) , train a meta-agent via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or reinforcement learning (RL) to autonomously generate MAS configurations. While more adaptive to different queries, these approaches need extensive data and struggle to generalize.\nL102: Moreover, most existing works neglect execution-time adaptability, leading to brittle open-loop pipelines.\nL103: ### 2.2 Learning at Test Time for LLMs\nL104: Learning at test-time refers to techniques that enable LLMs to adapt or improve their behavior during inference, without updating model parameters. These methods allow an LLM to leverage external knowledge, adjust its reasoning strategy, or correct its own outputs on the fly. For example, in-context learning (ICL) cite68†Dong et al. (2024) allows LLMs to infer task structure from demonstrations directly placed in the prompt. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) cite69†Lewis et al.\nL105: (2020) leverages relevant documents or memories retrieved at test time to fill knowledge gaps and guide generation. Test-time scaling methods cite70†Snell et al. (2024) expand LLM’s inference-time computation—such as sampling or reasoning path exploration—to improve accuracy for individual instances. In addition, test-time adaptation techniques cite71†Wang et al. (2020) aim at adapting a pre-trained model from the source domain to unlabeled data in the target domain.\nL106: While these techniques explore test-time learning for single LLM agents, test-time learning in MAS remains largely underexplored.\nL107: ### 2.3 Memory in Multi-Agent System\nL108: Memory plays a vital role in enabling LLM agents to accumulate experiences and enhance reasoning capabilities cite72†Wang et al. (2024a) . Research on single-agent memory has advanced considerably, evolving from simple RAG-style context extensions cite73†Packer et al. (2023) to cognitively inspired architectures that support cross-trial learning cite74†Xu et al. (2025) ; cite75†Wang et al. (2024b) . However, memory mechanisms tailored for MAS remain largely underexplored cite76†Tang et al. (2025) .\nL109: Recent efforts cite77†Zhang et al. (2025a) have attempted to manage MAS interaction histories and experiences via hierarchical graph structures, offering external memory banks for different MASs.\nL110: ## 3 Proposed Method\nL111: ### 3.1 Overall Framework\nL112: \nL113: The design of MASFly is motivated by the need for MAS to move from static, one-size-fits-all configurations to adaptive, reliable, and continuously improving collaboration at test time. To achieve this, MASFly draws inspiration from biological adaptation principles of phenotypic plasticity (i.e., the ability to express different traits based on environmental cues), and homeostatic regulation (the real-time adjustment capacity to maintain stability).\nL114: Inspired by these principles, MASFly operates through a closed-loop, three-stage process, as illustrated in Figure cite78†2 : (1) SOP-Guided System Generation, (2) Process Supervised Execution, and (3) Reflective Experience Distillation. MASFly begins with dynamic system generation via a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism.\nL115: Treating the SOP repository as a conserved “genetic toolkit” containing successful collaboration patterns, MASFly employs a hybrid retrieval strategy to identify query-related patterns. An SOP Agent then adaptively “expresses” these retrieved units, instantiating a query-specific MAS. During execution, MASFly applies an experience-guided process supervision mechanism.\nL116: Acting as a regulator, a global Watcher agent continuously monitors the system with the reference to a personalized experience pool, and dynamically adjusts agents when abnormal behaviors are detected. After execution, the LLM reflects on the outcome. It then either summarizes effective SOP to update the repository, or refines the system and records lessons from failures for future execution.\nL117: Through this design, MASFly not only adapts its structure to immediate tasks but also continuously enhances its resilience and capability for future collaborations.\nL118: ### 3.2 SOP-Guided System Generation\nL119: Constructing adaptive and high-quality MAS solely from an LLM’s internal knowledge is challenging, as the design space is vast and models often lack task-specific priors cite58†Ye et al. (2025) . In nature, biological development navigates environmental complexity using a conserved genetic toolkit—encoding evolutionarily proven patterns while allowing for flexible expression across different contexts.\nL120: Mirroring this strategy, MASFly introduces a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism, which leverages an SOP Repository that systematically records successful collaboration patterns, to guide the LLM in adpative system generation through RAG.\nL121: #### SOP Repository\nL122: To capture successful collaboration experience, we use LLMs to automatically construct a SOP repository using the labeled training data across diverse tasks (detailed in Section cite15†3.4 ). Each SOP in the repository is bound to a concrete case, enabling the system to retrieve suitable collaboration templates when facing new queries with analogous requirements. Specifically, as shown in Figure cite79†3 , each case $C_{i}=(Q_{i},N_{i},S_{i})$ in the repository is composed of three components:\nL123: \nL124:   * •\nL125: User Query $Q_{i}$: the problem statement;\nL126: \nL127:   * •\nL128: \nL129: Need Analysis $N_{i}$: the LLM-generated analysis about the objective and the capabilities required (such as specific tools or specialized agent roles);\nL130: \nL131:   * •\nL132: \nL133: SOP $S_{i}$: the corresponding multi-agent collaboration pattern, described through two parts:\nL134: \nL135:     * –\nL136: Agent Specifications: a set of agent definitions $\\{A_{i}=(E_{i},R_{i},I_{i},T_{i})\\}$, with $E_{i}$ the agent’s name, $R_{i}$ its responsibilities, $I_{i}$ its detailed instructions, and $T_{i}$ its available tools. For readability, all agent names are organized into a “Team” list at the start.\nL137: \nL138:     * –\nL139: \nL140: Communication Structure: a directed graph accompanied by textual descriptions of agent interactions, capturing how information flows between agents during execution.\nL141: Through this design, SOP Repository systematically records structured operational knowledge, allowing the system to reuse and recombine proven collaborative strategies across tasks.\nL142: #### Hybrid Retrieval and Instantiation\nL143: \nL144: Given a new query, MASFly aims to identify relevant collaboration patterns from SOP repository, leveraging past experience to generate a tailored MAS. Specifically, for the query $Q$, we first prompt an LLM to identify the need $N$, and then compute a hybrid retrieval score based on a weighted combination of external query similarity and internal need similarity to retrieve similar cases $C_{i}$ :\nL145:  | $\\displaystyle s(Q,C_{i})=\\;$  | $\\displaystyle\\lambda\\cdot\\text{Sim}(Q,Q_{i})$  |\nL146:  |  | $\\displaystyle+(1-\\lambda)\\cdot\\text{Sim}(N,N_{i}),$  |  | (1)\nL147: \nL148: where $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ is a hyperparameter that balances the contribution of the two components, and $\\text{Sim}(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the cosine similarity based on an embedding model.\nL149: The SOPs in the top-$K$ most similar cases $C_{i}$ are retrieved from the repository, guiding an LLM to dynamically generate an operation procedure (OP) that fits the current query:\nL150: \nL151:  | $$OP=\\text{LLM}(Q,N,\\{S_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{K}),$$  |  | (2)\nL152: where $OP$, structured similarly to an SOP, specifies the query-specific agent prompts and communication workflow. Consequently, MASFly transforms reusable procedural templates into concrete MASs, preserving effective coordination patterns while flexibly adapting to novel task requirements.\nL153: \nL154: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: The case illustration of the SOP Repository.\nL155: ### 3.3 Process Supervised Execution\nL156: While the OP provides a collaboration blueprint, the inherent complexity of MAS often leads to unexpected anomalies during execution, such as agent malfunction, tool misuse, or shifts in the external environment cite55†Chen et al. (2023) .\nL157: Analogous to how biological systems employ homeostatic regulation to maintain stability amidst internal and external fluctuations, MASFly designs an experience-guided process supervision mechanism, where a Watcher refers to a personalized experience pool, monitors and optimizes agent behavior in real-time, thus achieving adaptive and reliable execution.\nL158: #### Personalized Experience Pool\nL159: Effective supervision requires the ability to identify and handle typical anomalies. To this end, we construct a Personalized Experience Pool (PEP) through a reflection mechanism by an LLM (detailed in Section cite15†3.4 ), which stores common error patterns and their corresponding remedies, supporting the supervision module continuously refine agents behaviors.\nL160: As shown in Figure cite81†4 , the each record is formalized as $<Q_{i},F_{i},P_{i}>$, which comprises the User Query $Q_{i}$, the Failure Cause $F_{i}$ (e.g., task misallocation, tool misuse), and the set of Agent-Wise Experiences $P_{i}=\\{p_{1},p_{2},…,p_{m}\\}$, where each $p_{k}$ encapsulates an Error Attribution diagnosing the agent’s specific contribution to the failure and an Improvement Strategy proposing corrective insights to prevent future recurrence.\nL161: By organizing agent-level failure cases and improvement strategies from past executions, the PEP serves as a knowledge base for adaptive monitoring and real-time behavioral refinement.\nL162: cite82†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: The case illustration of the Personalized Experience Pool.\nL163: #### Process Supervision and Optimization\nL164: \nL165: During system execution, the supervision is carried out by a global Watcher Agent, which oversees system behavior, referencing both the OP and PEP. By retrieving relevant failure cases (indexed by the same queries as the SOP repository), the Watcher gains priors about potential pitfalls and uses them to implement dynamic, experience-informed interventions. Specifically, the Watcher conducts dual-level supervision:\nL166: \nL167:   * •\nL168: Inter-Agent Communication: verifying whether agents follow the prescribed workflow in the OP, maintain output consistency, and avoid unproductive conversational loops;\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: \nL172: Agent–Environment Interaction: assessing whether agents properly invoke designated tools, respond appropriately to dynamic contexts, and prevent repetitive or irrelevant outputs.\nL173: \nL174: When an anomaly is detected, the Watcher can take either of two possible actions for optimization:\nL175: \nL176:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Fluent to Verifiable: Claim-Level Auditability forDeep Research Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1)\nciteturn31895view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 329\nL80: This pipeline explains how agents generate reports, but it does not guarantee that each claim remains correct, supported, and traceable to evidence. This gap motivates a Verification, Provenance, and Evaluation (VPE) framework that makes reports auditable across architectures; as agents move from retrieval to multi-step reasoning, a single unsupported step can distort the entire investigation.\nL81: Existing approaches remain largely platform specific; proprietary systems add platform-specific checks, OpenAI uses reinforcement learning with grader models and secondary summarizers (e.g., o3-mini) to audit reasoning traces (cite48†Lightman et al., 2023 ), and Google introduces DeepSearchQA to benchmark factuality and report structure (cite49†Li et al., 2025 ).\nL82: Open-source efforts also advance standardization, including ReportBench (cite40†Sharma et al., 2025a ) for report-level evaluation and PROV AGENT (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ), which adapts W3C provenance standards (cite51†Moreau et al., 2013 ; cite28†Belhajjame et al., 2013 ) to track decision lineage. However, the field still lacks a unified, interoperable VPE framework that guarantees reliability and traceability across systems, so deployment often relies on trust rather than auditable proof.\nL83: ### 2.2. Why current provenance fails\nL84: Current provenance standards help reproduce a workflow. The W3C PROV standard records entities, activities, and agents to show how a system produces an output (cite52†Moreau and Groth, 2013 ; cite28†Belhajjame et al., 2013 ). Tools such as MLflow, DVC, and FAIR data pipeline use this idea to track data, code, and artifacts across runs (cite53†Mitchell et al., 2022 ). This record shows what the system runs. It does not show why a research claim is correct.\nL85: It also does not store clear links between each claim and the evidence that supports it (cite52†Moreau and Groth, 2013 ). Agent logs add more detail about tool use and retrieval. They record queries, retrieved papers, and executed code (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ; cite54†Souza et al., 2024 ). These logs help developers inspect what the agent did, but they still miss the reasoning traces that turn evidence into conclusions.\nL86: Structured memory approaches (cite55†Zhu et al., 2025a ; cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ) maintain persistent state across execution rounds, yet focus on forward continuity (what to search next) rather than backward traceability (which evidence supports each claim). Rerunning the same steps does not fix this issue because the model may make different choices during inference and the log does not capture those choices (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ).\nL87: This makes it hard to check if the agent reads sources correctly and uses fair baselines (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ). It also makes it hard to check if the experiment really tests the target idea (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). This gap makes audits harder and can spread weak results at scale (cite56†Blau et al., 2024 ; cite57†Pellegrina et al., 2025 ; cite58†Shumailov et al., 2024a ).\nL88: ## 3. Architectural failures in autonomous discovery\nL89: \nL90: {forest}\nL91: Figure 2. Taxonomy of architectural failures in deep research agents. Planning failures include objective drift and baseline rediscovery (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ; cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ). Execution failures include critical information loss over long tasks with substantial failure rates (cite59†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ).\nL92: Synthesis failures include citations that become disconnected from their sources, leading to high hallucination rates (cite60†Chelli et al., 2024a ; cite58†Shumailov et al., 2024a ). Model characteristics amplify these failures differently depending on model size and whether the model is open-source or proprietary (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025b ; cite62†Wolfe et al., 2024 ).\nL93: Deep research agents separate the execution environment (doing zone) from the reasoning environment (thinking zone) (cite63†Bhambri et al., 2025 ; cite64†Liao and Vaughan, 2024 ). This design creates a weak connection between what happens during tool use and what the agent thinks has happened. Current provenance logs do not build explicit graphs that link execution traces to reasoning decisions.\nL94: As a result, these systems do not provide the verification guarantees needed for autonomous scientific inquiry (cite65†Fernsel et al., 2024 ). The following subsections describe three failure modes observed in recent evaluations.\nL95: ### 3.1. Planning failures\nL96: A core architectural failure is that agents optimize for task completion rather than truth preservation (cite57†Pellegrina et al., 2025 ; cite56†Blau et al., 2024 ). Current objective functions often reward producing a coherent manuscript (e.g., a compiled PDF) instead of detecting when empirical outcomes contradict the stated goal (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ). This mismatch typically arises from three linked steps.\nL97: The plan simplifies the objective, the evaluation follows the simplified metric, and the review checks the write-up rather than the link between the objective and the evidence.\nL98: Objective drift: The AI Scientist report includes a task framed as improving energy efficiency in neural network training (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). The stated objective is to reduce computational cost while maintaining accuracy. The agent implements a new training schedule, runs benchmarks, and collects accuracy and compute measurements. The results show higher accuracy but also higher cost (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). This directly conflicts with the objective.\nL99: However, the final manuscript still presents the outcome as improved training efficiency using accuracy as the main evidence (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ; cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ). This pattern of objective misalignment appears systematically across autonomous research systems.\nL100: Evaluations of language model agents demonstrate goal drift scores ranging from 0.25 to 0.93 when exposed to competing objectives (cite66†Arike et al., 2025 ), while studies of 16 major AI models reveal that agents consistently bypass stated constraints to pursue alternative goals (cite67†Anthropic, 2025 ).\nL101: Prior work (cite68†Singh, 2026 ) also shows that in long-horizon tasks, agent state can drift and remain misaligned even when individual reasoning steps appear locally coherent, and that increasing context capacity alone does not prevent this drift.\nL102: Metric misalignment: The reviewer module of the deep research agent checks whether the paper is internally consistent (e.g., methods match results) but does not verify that the reported metrics test the stated objective. This requires a provenance chain that links the objective (reduce compute) to the chosen metrics (accuracy only) and to the acceptance decision (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ; cite52†Moreau and Groth, 2013 ).\nL103: Without this linkage, debate-style review cannot surface the error if both agents share the same metric mismatch (cite47†Du et al., 2023 ). The root cause is that the system has no way to remember and enforce the compute constraint throughout the workflow (cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ; cite46†Rasmussen and Team, 2025 ). Worryingly, the authors in (cite69†Naik et al., 2025 ) find that more capable agents are more prone to misalignment, and that persona characteristics can strongly and unpredictably shape this tendency.\nL104: Work on scientific integrity and transparency motivates architectures that make these links traceable and accountable (cite56†Blau et al., 2024 ; cite64†Liao and Vaughan, 2024 ).\nL105: Novelty verification failures: The AI Scientist reports micro-batching for stochastic gradient descent algorithm as a novel contribution (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). This technique is standard in model training, but simplistic keyword-driven retrieval fails to map equivalent terminology.\nL106: Without persistent knowledge structures tracking previously explored hypotheses (cite55†Zhu et al., 2025a ; cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ), agents cannot recognize when they revisit known solutions, contributing to the broader problem of model collapse where AI-generated content contaminates training data (cite58†Shumailov et al., 2024a ).\nL107: Related evidence (cite70†Wang et al., 2025 ) shows that language-based agents also struggle to adapt when feedback changes the environment: they may select the right tool initially, but they often fail to revise plans, explore alternatives, or execute contingency strategies even in constrained search spaces.\nL108: ### 3.2. Execution failures\nL109: The cross-validation implementation bug: Autonomous research requires the initial experimental specification to remain available and is enforced throughout planning, implementation, execution, and reporting (cite59†Liu et al., 2024 ). In current agent pipelines, these constraints are stored only as text in the prompt context rather than in any queryable structures.\nL110: Long trajectories, limited context windows and the lost in the middle effect reduce the likelihood that earlier requirements are attended to during later edits and decisions (cite59†Liu et al., 2024 ) (see Figure cite71†3 , left). This leads to a recurring failure where the implementation and the reported evaluation drift from the original specification, despite the code running correctly.\nL111: The AI Scientist provides an instance where a cross-validation plan specifies multiple $k$ values, but later code revisions effectively test only one setting while the final write-up still describes the broader evaluation (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ). The underlying issue is not only code correctness but specification–implementation consistency since the system lacks a persistent representation that binds experimental requirements to concrete checks in code and results (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ).\nL112: A very recent work (cite72†Li et al., 2026 ) shows that strong key performance indicators (KPI) performance can coincide with violating mandated constraints, exposing drift between what agents optimize and what the specification requires (e.g., ethical or safety limits). Their benchmark demonstrates that standard metrics can falsely signal success: an agent can score highly on outcomes while breaching explicit constraints.\nL113: Across 12 state-of-the-art LLMs, they measure outcome-driven constraint violations from 1.3% to 71.4%, with Gemini-3-Pro-Preview showing the highest violation rate at 71.4% While trajectory compression methods (cite55†Zhu et al., 2025a ) extend effective context through structured state summaries, they compress for forward continuity rather than backward verification. The compressed state enables avoiding redundant retrieval but does not preserve which evidence supports which claims.\nL114: Transient memory as architectural liability: Persistent memory and trace structures target this gap (Figure cite71†3 , right). Work on knowledge-graph-based memory shows benefits for multi-step reasoning and coordination compared to stateless prompting (cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ; cite73†Bernal et al., 2024 ). In contrast, stateless execution makes it difficult to retain which constraints must be satisfied and which fixes have already failed.\nL115: High execution failure rate: Consistent with this, the AI Scientist evaluation reports that 5 out of 12 proposed experiments fail to execute (42%) due to unresolved coding errors (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ), and multi-agent studies report coordination failures that include losing track of prior attempts (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ).\nL116: Provenance-style traces can record constraints, attempted fixes, and outcomes so the agent can detect repeated failures and escalate earlier when needed (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ; cite74†Koohestani and others, 2025 ). Complementary evidence (cite75†Song et al., 2025 ) shows that many execution failures arise from agent-environment interaction rather than planning alone. Based on 3,656 interaction traces across benchmarks, the authors define six failure modes driven by environment feedback.\nL117: Figure 3. Stateless vs. Cumulative Memory Architectures. Left: Current stateless execution. Critical constraints (e.g., “$k\\in\\{2,3,4,5\\}$”) specified at Step 1 become inaccessible by Step 15 when code is revised due to context window limitations (cite59†Liu et al., 2024 ). Right: Proposed cumulative memory.\nL118: A persistent constraint graph (cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ; cite46†Rasmussen and Team, 2025 ) maintains the specification as a structured requirement, enabling automated verification that implementation satisfies all original constraints (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ).\nL119: ### 3.3. Synthesis failures\nL120: Synthesis failures occur when agents generate fluent text with apparently valid citations, but the cited sources do not actually support the stated claims.\nL121: The architectural root cause: Deep research agents risk producing black box science (cite76†Lipton, 2018 ; cite63†Bhambri et al., 2025 ) unlike human scientists who ground claims in specific passages that logically support their conclusions. Agents generate text through opaque vector operations where citations function as plausibility signals rather than evidential links (cite77†Wang et al., 2024a ).\nL122: This citation decorrelation, i.e., the systematic disconnect between cited papers and the claims they purport to support, arises from the architecture of retrieval systems that operate through cosine similarity in embedding space rather than formal logical inference (cite60†Chelli et al., 2024a ; cite78†Metropolitansky and Larson, 2025 ).\nL123: Retrieval mechanisms surface papers that are topically similar to a claim based on high cosine similarity without encoding the logical relationship between evidence and conclusion.\nL124: Geometrically, a claim such as “Treatment X reduces symptom Y” retrieves literature mentioning treatment X and symptom Y regardless of whether the paper demonstrates efficacy, refutes it, or studies an unrelated mechanism, because shared entities dominate embedding similarity while evidential directionality is ignored (cite79†Bowman et al., 2015 ).\nL125: DeepTRACE (cite80†Venkit et al., 2026 ) shows that deep research agents often generate strongly one-sided and overly confident answers to debate-style questions, while a substantial proportion of their claims are not actually supported by the sources they themselves cite. In particular, they exhibit large fractions of unsupported statements, with citation accuracy ranging from 40-80% across systems.\nL126: ChemCrow (cite39†Bran et al., 2024 ; cite57†Pellegrina et al., 2025 ) illustrates this failure mode where agents planned correct chemical syntheses but attributed reaction conditions to papers that used different catalysts or substrates, producing citations that appeared relevant but were evidentially false. Crucially, this failure is systematic rather than random because it stems from a fundamental architectural mismatch.\nL127: Logical support has directional structure: if paper A proves “catalyst X enables reaction Y,” then A supports the claim but the claim does not inversely prove A’s methodology. Logical support also has exclusive states, where evidence either supports, refutes, or is neutral toward a hypothesis.\nL128: In contrast, cosine similarity is symmetric (the distance from A to B equals B to A) and blending (contradictory evidence vectors average rather than conflict), making it mathematically incapable of representing entailment or contradiction (cite81†Wang et al., 2024b ; cite82†Manakul et al., 2023 ). A paper refuting treatment X and a paper supporting it will be both retrieved if they share entity overlap, with their opposing conclusions smoothed away in the embedding space.\nL129: These errors reflect fundamental mathematical incompatibilities rather than limitations fixable through scale or better training.\nL130: The unverifiable inference chain: In documented evaluations of The AI Scientist (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ), the agent produced a report claiming improved training efficiency despite experimental results showing increased computational cost (23% more FLOPs, 18% more wall-clock time).\nL131: The system cannot answer verification questions such as “Which evidence justified framing higher accuracy as efficiency?” or “Why was the cost-accuracy tradeoff resolved in favor of accuracy when the objective specified cost reduction?” because the reasoning occurred through transient neural activations during LLM forward passes rather than explicit logical operations (cite63†Bhambri et al., 2025 ).\nL132: The final report contains no record of how the agent reconciled contradictory objectives or weighted competing metrics. This reflects a fundamental gap: while execution logs capture what code ran and what results emerged, they cannot recover why the agent interpreted those results as supporting the original claim, because that interpretation happened in vector space rather than through traceable logical steps (cite77†Wang et al., 2024a ; cite63†Bhambri et al., 2025 ).\nL133: Current research agents provide no reconstructible trace linking generated claims to supporting evidence through explicit reasoning steps (cite50†Souza et al., 2025 ). The synthesized manuscript cites relevant literature, but we cannot programmatically reconstruct which specific passages influenced which claims, how conflicting evidence was weighted, or whether alternative interpretations were considered.\n"
